1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,991
A proof of the Riemann hypothesis has recently been checked in Lean.

2
00:00:04,252 --> 00:00:07,383
Independent programs have accepted the formal proof.

3
00:00:07,643 --> 00:00:11,887
It concerns one of the most famous questions about the pattern of
prime numbers.

4
00:00:12,148 --> 00:00:14,165
That is a remarkable development.

5
00:00:14,426 --> 00:00:16,154
But it leaves another question.

6
00:00:16,414 --> 00:00:20,635
How much mathematical machinery does this particular argument really
need?

7
00:00:20,896 --> 00:00:27,481
To explain what Landau Windows ACL Two is trying to do, we need to
look at the foundations underneath a proof.

8
00:00:29,367 --> 00:00:31,050
Start with the whole numbers.

9
00:00:31,316 --> 00:00:34,030
Zero, one, two, three, and so on.

10
00:00:34,297 --> 00:00:38,362
Peano arithmetic is a precise system of rules about those numbers.

11
00:00:38,629 --> 00:00:43,346
It tells us how the next number works, and how addition and
multiplication behave.

12
00:00:43,613 --> 00:00:45,438
It also gives us induction.

13
00:00:45,704 --> 00:00:47,684
Imagine a row of dominoes.

14
00:00:47,950 --> 00:00:53,746
Establish the first case, then show that whenever one case holds,
the next one holds too.

15
00:00:54,013 --> 00:00:58,042
Together, those two steps prove the property for every whole number.

16
00:00:58,309 --> 00:01:00,999
We never have to check the numbers one at a time.

17
00:01:01,266 --> 00:01:06,267
Arithmetic can also encode finite lists, fractions, and the steps of
a calculation.

18
00:01:06,534 --> 00:01:10,884
So this small starting point can express a surprising amount of
mathematics.

19
00:01:12,767 --> 00:01:15,149
Now give ourselves a larger framework.

20
00:01:15,416 --> 00:01:19,125
ZFC is a standard collection of rules for set theory.

21
00:01:19,392 --> 00:01:22,319
A set is a collection of mathematical objects.

22
00:01:22,586 --> 00:01:29,152
The rules let us build new sets, work with infinite sets, and
develop a great deal of ordinary mathematics.

23
00:01:29,419 --> 00:01:32,061
Even the whole numbers can be built out of sets.

24
00:01:32,328 --> 00:01:38,183
We can start with the empty set, then build a sequence representing
zero, one, two, and so on.

25
00:01:38,450 --> 00:01:43,356
Inside this framework we can also construct functions, real numbers,
and spaces.

26
00:01:43,623 --> 00:01:47,510
That is one reason ZFC is stronger than Peano arithmetic.

27
00:01:47,777 --> 00:01:51,890
It can describe and justify much more than the arithmetic rules
alone.

28
00:01:53,767 --> 00:01:55,983
Why does the strength of the rules matter?

29
00:01:56,250 --> 00:01:58,940
First, we need a word: consistency.

30
00:01:59,207 --> 00:02:03,924
A consistent system never proves a contradiction, such as zero
equals one.

31
00:02:04,190 --> 00:02:07,035
Checking a particular proof is a finite task.

32
00:02:07,301 --> 00:02:12,528
But saying that no proof of a contradiction exists is a statement
about every possible proof.

33
00:02:12,795 --> 00:02:14,999
There is a remarkable connection here.

34
00:02:15,266 --> 00:02:18,656
A finite proof can itself be encoded as a whole number.

35
00:02:18,922 --> 00:02:21,802
That lets arithmetic make statements about proofs.

36
00:02:22,069 --> 00:02:26,762
In nineteen thirty one, Gödel established his second incompleteness
theorem.

37
00:02:27,029 --> 00:02:35,444
Under its usual conditions, a consistent system with mechanically
specified rules and enough arithmetic cannot prove its own formal
consistency.

38
00:02:35,710 --> 00:02:39,005
This applies to Peano arithmetic, and to ZFC.

39
00:02:39,272 --> 00:02:47,497
So a system can check individual arguments without being able to
establish, from its own rules, that those rules will never produce a
contradiction.

40
00:02:49,367 --> 00:02:53,728
A stronger system can sometimes provide that justification for a
weaker one.

41
00:02:53,995 --> 00:02:57,147
Here is how ZFC does it for Peano arithmetic.

42
00:02:57,414 --> 00:03:04,383
It constructs the whole numbers, together with addition and
multiplication, and checks that they satisfy the arithmetic axioms.

43
00:03:04,650 --> 00:03:09,355
This is called building a model: a mathematical world where the
rules hold.

44
00:03:09,621 --> 00:03:12,632
Valid proof steps preserve truth in that world.

45
00:03:12,899 --> 00:03:17,011
So an arithmetic proof cannot lead from those rules to a
contradiction.

46
00:03:17,278 --> 00:03:22,587
That gives a proof of the consistency of Peano arithmetic, carried
out inside ZFC.

47
00:03:22,854 --> 00:03:25,805
The justification depends on the stronger framework.

48
00:03:26,072 --> 00:03:31,026
When ZFC asks about its own consistency, Gödel's limit still
applies.

49
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:35,519
Now repeat that idea one level higher.

50
00:03:35,786 --> 00:03:38,358
Not all infinities have the same size.

51
00:03:38,624 --> 00:03:44,076
An inaccessible cardinal is a special size of infinity with a useful
feature for our story.

52
00:03:44,343 --> 00:03:49,878
If one exists, the sets below it form a mathematical world
satisfying ZFC.

53
00:03:50,144 --> 00:03:55,442
So assuming such a cardinal exists gives us a way to prove that ZFC
is consistent.

54
00:03:55,709 --> 00:03:57,475
But we just met the obstacle.

55
00:03:57,741 --> 00:04:02,020
If ZFC is consistent, it cannot prove its own consistency.

56
00:04:02,287 --> 00:04:06,601
Therefore it cannot prove that an inaccessible cardinal exists,
either.

57
00:04:06,867 --> 00:04:11,976
If it could, the model we just described would supply the forbidden
consistency proof.

58
00:04:12,242 --> 00:04:17,576
Accepting an inaccessible cardinal is therefore an additional
assumption beyond ZFC.

59
00:04:17,842 --> 00:04:22,808
This is why reducing the assumptions behind a theorem is a
meaningful mathematical goal.

60
00:04:23,075 --> 00:04:28,254
We want to understand exactly how much of this larger framework the
argument actually needs.

61
00:04:30,133 --> 00:04:33,724
A proof of the Riemann hypothesis has now been checked in Lean.

62
00:04:33,991 --> 00:04:40,759
The usual set theoretic models of Lean's universe hierarchy use ZFC
together with inaccessible cardinals.

63
00:04:41,025 --> 00:04:45,991
Yet the Riemann hypothesis itself can be stated entirely in terms of
whole numbers.

64
00:04:46,258 --> 00:04:51,105
The larger proof draws on substantial mathematics developed in that
stronger setting.

65
00:04:51,372 --> 00:04:55,627
We have focused on one essential part: the classical Landau bridge.

66
00:04:55,893 --> 00:05:02,921
This encoded windowed ceiling reduction has now been proved in Peano
Arithmetic and certified in vanilla ACL2.

67
00:05:03,188 --> 00:05:10,299
The direct arithmetic proof establishes this Landau conversion with
fewer foundational assumptions than Lean's ambient framework.

68
00:05:10,566 --> 00:05:13,730
This is the result of Landau Windows ACL Two.

69
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:20,425
To see which piece we are studying, return to the picture from the
original video.

70
00:05:20,704 --> 00:05:26,546
The dots are zeros of the zeta function, and the Riemann hypothesis
puts them on the central line.

71
00:05:26,825 --> 00:05:28,561
There is a mirror symmetry.

72
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:32,040
An off-centre dot on the left would have a partner on the right.

73
00:05:32,319 --> 00:05:34,887
So ruling out dots on the right is enough.

74
00:05:36,767 --> 00:05:41,341
Now watch the gap: the difference between a weighted prime count and
its prediction.

75
00:05:41,608 --> 00:05:47,060
If a zero sits too far to the right, it contributes an oscillation
that grows too quickly.

76
00:05:47,327 --> 00:05:49,673
That extra wave rises and falls.

77
00:05:49,940 --> 00:05:53,069
Eventually it would drive the gap above the allowed level.

78
00:05:53,336 --> 00:05:57,294
Now suppose we could prove an upper bound that the gap never
crosses.

79
00:05:57,561 --> 00:06:00,227
Then that troublesome zero could not be there.

80
00:06:00,494 --> 00:06:04,749
We only used a ceiling; a matching lower bound was unnecessary.

81
00:06:05,016 --> 00:06:07,836
Landau's theorem makes that reasoning rigorous.

82
00:06:08,103 --> 00:06:11,753
In the larger proof, another argument supplies the ceiling.

83
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:17,709
This connection between a one sided bound and a zero free region is
the bridge we want to extract.

84
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:21,768
Here is the piece we have extracted.

85
00:06:22,029 --> 00:06:27,258
Landau takes a bound on the prime counting error and converts it
into a statement about zeros.

86
00:06:27,519 --> 00:06:34,452
We have proved the encoded ceiling conversion in Peano Arithmetic
and certified the corresponding development in ACL2.

87
00:06:34,713 --> 00:06:37,925
The input ceiling still has to be established separately.

88
00:06:38,186 --> 00:06:45,096
The theorem computes a window threshold and returns a certified
rational violation under its stated zero hypotheses.

89
00:06:46,967 --> 00:06:52,099
Our version only gets the ceiling on recurring stretches of the
number line, called windows.

90
00:06:52,365 --> 00:06:58,019
A zero in the forbidden region must eventually force a violation in
sufficiently large windows.

91
00:06:58,285 --> 00:07:03,927
The effective theorem should compute where that violation happens,
and certify it with exact bounds.

92
00:07:04,193 --> 00:07:10,487
So the goal includes both finding a witness and proving that the
search succeeds under the intended hypotheses.

93
00:07:12,367 --> 00:07:18,268
We used ACL2, a proof system based on a computational logic for
applicative Common Lisp.

94
00:07:18,529 --> 00:07:20,929
Common Lisp is its programming language.

95
00:07:21,190 --> 00:07:24,900
The foundational issue is the logical rules used by this proof.

96
00:07:25,161 --> 00:07:31,943
Our calculations use finite lists, exact fractions, and recursive
functions whose properties are proved by induction.

97
00:07:32,204 --> 00:07:36,170
The corresponding development is certified in vanilla ACL2.

98
00:07:36,430 --> 00:07:43,943
A direct manuscript proof derives the encoded theorem in first-order
Peano Arithmetic, including its threshold and rational witness.

99
00:07:44,204 --> 00:07:50,941
The manuscript supplies the mathematical proof, while the ACL2
certificates record the machine-checked development.

100
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:56,046
That is what Landau Windows ACL Two has accomplished.

101
00:07:56,307 --> 00:08:06,441
The encoded ceiling reduction now has a direct Peano Arithmetic
proof, a computed threshold, a certified rational witness, and its
connection to the original Landau theorem.

102
00:08:06,701 --> 00:08:11,177
This encoded windowed Landau ceiling reduction is proved in Peano
Arithmetic.

103
00:08:11,438 --> 00:08:13,177
Landau was our starting point.

104
00:08:13,438 --> 00:08:18,006
Other parts of the larger argument may also be reducible to Peano
arithmetic.

105
00:08:18,267 --> 00:08:23,588
Whether a full arithmetic proof of the Riemann hypothesis can be
found remains a separate question.

106
00:08:23,849 --> 00:08:26,829
This project does not settle those wider questions.

107
00:08:27,090 --> 00:08:32,643
Here we have completed one essential conversion, with its hypotheses
and interpretation written down.

108
00:08:32,904 --> 00:08:35,328
The same result, with fewer assumptions.
